Make /etc/ld.so.preload follow --sysconfdir, and let it be redirected.

WHAT IS WRONG TODAY

rtld.c names the host's /etc/ld.so.preload as a literal, so a payload loader
reads the HOST's preload list and loads the host objects on it into every
process it starts.  That is every process we start: the compiler, its
cc1plus/as/ld children, and the binaries we ship to users.  The host objects
are built against the host's libc, ours is a different one, and neither
outcome is visible to the program:

  * the preloaded object's own dependency is not in our closure, so the
    process dies at startup naming a library nothing we built ever referenced
      .../bin/g++: error while loading shared libraries: libdl.so.2:
      cannot open shared object file: No such file or directory
  * or it is self-contained, and then it simply runs against our libc
      LD_DEBUG=libs: libclean.so -> .../xim-x-glibc/2.44/lib64/libc.so.6
    which is the "one process, two libcs" case the ecosystem-closure design
    exists to prevent, arriving through a door nobody was watching

Measured, mcpp-community/mcpp#484: a host with /etc/ld.so.preload cannot run
`g++ --version`, cannot compile, cannot link, and cannot run the binaries we
produce (exit 127); a host without one is unaffected, which is why this went
unseen -- the file is empty on dev boxes and CI, and present on exactly the
audited, managed and cloud hosts our users deploy to.

The private ld.so.cache does not save us: its path is compiled in too, and it
names the build machine.  There is no --inhibit-preload; ld.so has one for the
cache and one for RPATH and deliberately none for this, because
/etc/ld.so.preload is the channel that survives secure-execution mode.

WHY THIS SHAPE

Upstream already relocates the sibling path -- LD_SO_CACHE is
SYSCONFDIR "/ld.so.cache" in dl-cache.h -- and leaves this one literal.  So
this is not a switch bolted onto glibc; it is the same treatment the cache
already gets.  For --sysconfdir=/etc, which is what a system glibc is built
with, the two spellings are the same string and this patch is a no-op.

A build-time prefix cannot be the whole answer for a package whose install
path is not its build path, so XLINGS_LD_PRELOAD_FILE redirects it at run
time; empty means "no preload file".  It is added to unsecvars.h, which is
what makes it behave like LD_PRELOAD under set-user-ID -- see the comment
there before touching it.

WHAT IT COSTS

Processes started by this loader no longer honour the host's preload list,
which is where audit / APM / HIDS agents inject themselves.  For a payload
that is the point.  It is also not a regression: on such a host those
processes do not run at all today.

--- a/elf/rtld.c
+++ b/elf/rtld.c
@@ -1834,8 +1834,40 @@
      open().  So we do this first.  If it succeeds we do almost twice
      the work but this does not matter, since it is not for production
      use.  */
-  static const char preload_file[] = "/etc/ld.so.preload";
-  if (__glibc_unlikely (__access (preload_file, R_OK) == 0))
+  /* xlings: this file follows --sysconfdir, the way ld.so.cache already
+     does, instead of naming the host's /etc unconditionally.
+
+     A relocatable payload is not the host system.  Reading the host's list
+     injects host-built objects -- compiled against a different libc -- into
+     every process this loader starts, which for us includes the compiler,
+     its cc1plus/as/ld children, and the binaries we ship to users.  When
+     such an object's own dependency is not reachable, the process dies at
+     startup naming a library nothing in our closure ever referenced; when
+     it is reachable, it silently runs against our libc instead of the one
+     it was built for.  Neither is visible to the program.
+
+     Upstream already relocates LD_SO_CACHE with SYSCONFDIR (dl-cache.h)
+     and leaves this path literal.  For --sysconfdir=/etc -- what a system
+     glibc is built with -- the two forms are the same string, so this is a
+     no-op for any ordinary build.
+
+     XLINGS_LD_PRELOAD_FILE redirects it at run time, which a build-time
+     prefix cannot do for a package whose install path is not its build
+     path.  An empty value means "no preload file at all".
+
+     The variable is listed in unsecvars.h, so process_envvars_secure() has
+     already removed it from the environment before we get here whenever
+     __libc_enable_secure is set -- the same treatment LD_PRELOAD gets, and
+     for the same reason.  The test below states that property here too,
+     rather than leaving it to a file three directories away.  */
+  const char *preload_file = SYSCONFDIR "/ld.so.preload";
+  {
+    const char *xlings_preload_file = getenv ("XLINGS_LD_PRELOAD_FILE");
+    if (xlings_preload_file != NULL && !__libc_enable_secure)
+      preload_file = xlings_preload_file;
+  }
+  if (preload_file[0] != '\0'
+      && __glibc_unlikely (__access (preload_file, R_OK) == 0))
     {
       /* Read the contents of the file.  */
       file = _dl_sysdep_read_whole_file (preload_file, &file_size,
--- a/sysdeps/generic/unsecvars.h
+++ b/sysdeps/generic/unsecvars.h
@@ -1,6 +1,24 @@
 /* Environment variable to be removed for SUID programs.  The names are
    all stuffed in a single string which means they have to be terminated
    with a '\0' explicitly.  */
+
+/* xlings: XLINGS_LD_PRELOAD_FILE at the end of this list is load-bearing, and
+   deleting it on a rebase is silent.
+
+   That variable names the file rtld.c reads instead of the compiled-in
+   preload path, so it can point at any file the caller likes -- the same
+   power LD_PRELOAD has, in file form.  LD_PRELOAD is in this list because a
+   set-user-ID program must not let its caller choose what gets loaded into
+   it; the file form needs the entry for exactly the same reason, and
+   dropping it turns every set-user-ID binary that runs under this loader
+   into an injection point.
+
+   Being listed here is what makes rtld.c's getenv() return NULL under
+   __libc_enable_secure: process_envvars_secure() unsets everything in this
+   string before dl_main() reaches the preload block.  It also removes the
+   name from the environment the program sees, which is the documented
+   contract for the rest of this list (see ld.so(8), "Secure-execution
+   mode") and the reason a local check in rtld.c is not a substitute.  */
 #define UNSECURE_ENVVARS \
   "GCONV_PATH\0"							      \
   "GETCONF_DIR\0"							      \
@@ -35,4 +53,5 @@
   "RESOLV_HOST_CONF\0"							      \
   "RES_OPTIONS\0"							      \
   "TMPDIR\0"								      \
-  "TZDIR\0"
+  "TZDIR\0"								      \
+  "XLINGS_LD_PRELOAD_FILE\0"
